Added a few extra notes at the beginning of the lecture tutorial:

For development purposes, we recommend using an Ubuntu virtual machine set up with Oracle VirtualBox. It is crucial to use a current LTS (Long Term Support) version of Ubuntu (e.g., 22.04 or 24.04) rather than outdated versions. (Current version is 26.04.1). Choose the Intel/AMD architecture unless you are on a machine with an ARM processor (i.e. Raspberry Pi). 64-bit (x86-64) host CPU and host OS required.

Installation
VirtualBox
VirtualBox is free and open-source. Detailed installation instructions for your specific operating system can be found at: * VirtualBox Downloads

Ubuntu ISO
Download the latest Ubuntu Desktop LTS image: * Ubuntu Desktop Download

Hardware Requirements
Depending on your host machine's specifications, you may find that some configurations are too resource-constrained. We recommend the following minimal host setup for a smooth experience: * CPU: Multi-core processor with virtualization extensions (VT-x or AMD-V) enabled in BIOS/UEFI. * RAM: At least 8 GB of system memory. * Storage: At least 50 GB of free hard drive space.

Recommended VM Configurations
Depending on your task, you may want to adjust the resources allocated to the VM:

Profile	CPU Cores	RAM	Disk Space	Use Case
Minimal	1	2 GB	10 GB	Basic Linux CLI tasks
Development	2	4 GB	25 GB	IDEs, Compiling, LaTeX
Heavy	4	8 GB	50 GB	Docker, Heavy Data Processing
A tutorial video for the installation process is available here: Video Using Ubuntu in VirtualBox

Step One:

Open VirtualBox and click the New (blue star) button in the top toolbar

Set Name to "Ubuntu"

Set Type to Linux and Version to Ubuntu (64-bit)

In the ISO Image field, browse and select the downloaded Ubuntu .iso file

You may be prompted for a username and password. If you add one, remember it because you'll need it for when you open Ubuntu

Check Skip Unattended Installation if you prefer to perform the standard OS setup manually, then click Next

Step Two:
Assign physical system resources to your virtual machine
  See information above for allocations

Assign at least 2 CPUs for smooth performance

Click Next

Step Three:
Allocate the virtual hard disk space based on the recommended amounts above

Click Next, review the summary and then click Finish

Step Four:
To boot your new VM, select from the left panel and click Start (green arrow)

The system will install and it might take a while. Be patient

The Ubuntu screen will come up when installation is complete and you will be prompted for UN/PW

Enter the information you added during setup and you will be logged in


Your VM is all set up! You can go from there with additional options and tools.



